// SPDX-License-Identifier: Apache-2.0
; specs/turbobaby/http_cache.t27 -- what a cached catalog answer is made of, how long a copy of
; it may be held, and what that holding time costs a price
; This contract owns three boundaries, and that no other file occupies them is a MEASUREMENT
; and not an impression: re-run 2026-09-21 over all 43 files of the corpus, with the two near
; misses named here rather than left for a reader to trip over. The three are the derivation of
; the entity tag the catalog list answers with, the lifetime of a copy a client is allowed to
; keep without asking again, and the price risk that lifetime creates.
; BOUNDARY ONE, the tag. Two contracts mention an entity tag: this one and
; turbobaby/catalog-api. That one takes etag_matches as a BOOLEAN PARAMETER of
; catalog_api_list_status and derives no tag from anything. The derivation is here and nowhere
; else, and the distinction is the whole reason both files can exist.
; BOUNDARY TWO, the lifetime. Two contracts declare a max-age. The other is
; turbobaby/request-identity, whose AUTH_DATE_MAX_AGE_SECONDS bounds the freshness of an
; initData auth stamp at 86400 seconds -- a different subject, a different clock, and not the
; lifetime of a served copy. A reader greping this corpus for a max-age will find that one, so
; this sentence is the answer to that grep rather than a claim it would refute.
; BOUNDARY THREE, the price risk. One contract, this one. Nothing else in the corpus relates an
; age to a price.
; turbobaby/catalog-api owns the conditional OUTCOME -- its catalog_api_list_status and
; catalog_api_response_has_body decide what the transport answers -- and this file restates no
; status code and declares no HTTP number at all. The two local discriminants below are named OUTCOME_NOT_MODIFIED and
; OUTCOME_FULL_BODY and carry the values 1 and 2 precisely so that nobody can mistake them for
; the wire.
; --
; OWNERSHIP EDGES, spelled as owner ID plus declaration name because this language has no import
; and because a line number into a neighbour rots. Every edge below was re-opened 2026-09-21;
; every declaration named exists and still means what the sentence claims. One of them proved
; the point while the check was running: DROP_MIGRATION moved from line 83 to line 95 inside its
; own file that morning, under a concurrent edit. The name resolved either way. A number would
; not have, and it would not have said so.
; turbobaby/pricing-honesty owns PRICE_AUTHORITY, the rule that the door is the price authority;
; this file owns only the observation that a number with an age is not that authority's answer
; NOW, and takes the door's decision as a boolean.
; turbobaby/availability owns what a unit count means and what may confirm one -- available_count
; computes it, FILE_MAY_CONFIRM and CONFIRMATION_AUTHORITY say what may not and what may; this
; file observes that the same body carries a unit count and therefore ages it identically.
; turbobaby/legacy-retirement owns the census of what migration 083 retired, in DROP_MIGRATION;
; this file names one consequence of that census and republishes no table list.
; turbobaby/rate-limit owns attempt_admitted and is the only place a bound on the key growth
; described below could come from; that edge was added after a grep, not from the brief.
; --
; WHY (measured 2026-09-21). The number is 60. src/api/bikes.rs:516-519 sends the catalog list
; with a cache directive whose age budget is 60 seconds, and that is the only freshness number
; anybody in this repository has set for a catalog answer. Against it, DECISIONS.md:156-170 --
; D11, the ruling that makes the door the sole price authority and forbids invention and silence
; equally -- contains no freshness clause: grep -in "stale\|freshness\|price age\|acceptable age"
; DECISIONS.md returns 0 lines. So a client may hold, and act on, a catalog body up to 59 whole
; seconds after the origin built it, and no decision in this repository says whether that is
; acceptable for a price. The author of the call site saw this and wrote it down rather than
; choosing: src/api/bikes.rs:483-488 records that once a door number is in the body the tag
; stops being a pure content hash, that a cached body then serves a stale door price for up to
; the max-age, and that issue #25 does not specify which behaviour is wanted. That comment is a
; decision deferred in a source file where nothing will ever check it. This contract is where it
; becomes checkable.
; --
; AND IT IS DECIDED, THOUGH NOT HERE AND NOT IN SECONDS (read 2026-09-21). The paragraph above
; was written against this repository and is still true of it. It is no longer the whole truth,
; because the owner's knowledge base -- the brain -- publishes a rule for exactly this class of
; value, and the rule was read today. It is cited the way a source file is cited, by node and
; line: brain:knowledge_base:856-866. What it rules is not a ceiling. It rules that a number has
; THREE outcomes rather than two, that a figure from a cache may be served while it says so and
; says when it was true, and that where there is nothing to serve, nothing is what gets shown. So
; the seconds refusal below stands untouched and the silence around it does not: the section "What
; a number from a cache must call itself" carries the published rule, the incident it came from,
; the date it was decided, and the two places this contract declines to extend it.
; --
; WHERE THE BRIEF IS REFUTED, five times, all measured 2026-09-21.
; FIRST, and it moves the whole subject. The brief asks how "a cached body" is derived. No body
; is cached in this process. src/api/cache.rs:22 declares the map as String -> String: a key to a
; sixty-four-character hex digest, and nothing else. src/api/cache.rs:95-128 stores that digest
; and src/api/bikes.rs:499 hands it the body it has just built at :478. The origin therefore
; remembers a HASH and never a BODY. The only cached body in this system lives in the client,
; held there by the directive at src/api/bikes.rs:516-519, and it is reachable by no server-side
; action whatever. Everything the brief calls "the cache" is two different things with two
; different owners, and conflating them is how an invalidation gets believed to recall an answer.
; SECOND. The brief cites the call site as "around :481-515". The observation it quotes is at
; :486 and inside that span, but the max-age the observation depends on is at :516-519 and
; outside it. The span given ends one line before the number that makes the comment true.
; THIRD. The brief asks for "the invalidation rule (or its absence)". Both are true at once and
; neither alone is honest. Four invalidators exist -- src/api/cache.rs:160-164, :168-170, :174-176
; and :180-182 -- naming five keys between them. The live catalog writer creates keys beginning
; "bikes" or "bikes_available" (src/api/bikes.rs:489-498), and not one of the five is such a key.
; Three of the four carry a dead-code annotation and a Russian comment (src/api/cache.rs:166-167,
; :172-173, :178-179, cited by path and not quoted) saying they await a future path. The fourth is
; called exactly once, at src/api/admin.rs:686, whose own comment at :683-685 names the endpoint
; it is clearing for -- an endpoint src/ui/api/client.rs:156-158 (was :119-121) records as retired by migration
; 083. So invalidation coverage of the live keys is zero, and it is harmless: correctness comes
; from src/api/cache.rs:120 re-hashing the current body on every call, which is exactly what the
; module docstring at src/api/bikes.rs:10-13 claims. That claim is true and it is about the
; origin only; it says nothing about the 60 seconds.
; The line numbers in this paragraph moved on 2026-09-21 and the facts did not: the repair
; described under THE TAG below lengthened src/api/cache.rs from 150 lines to 314. Four
; invalidators, five key names, one call site, zero live keys -- all re-counted after the edit.
; FOURTH. The brief says a cached body can serve a stale door price. Today it cannot, and the
; reason is worth a constant rather than a footnote: src/api/bikes.rs:250-253 is a stub that
; answers every key with silence, so no door number has ever entered a body. The window is armed
; and empty. The day issue #25 wires the door, a 60-second price age appears with no code change
; at the cache at all -- which is the precise event this contract exists to make loud.
; FIFTH. The brief says to claim no coherence across replicas, and that refusal stands, but the
; repository supplies a measurement the brief did not. railway.toml:14-18 declares one region
; with numReplicas = 1, and src/main.rs:715 constructs exactly one map per process. So the map is
; coherent today by deployment and not by design. What a second replica would cost is also
; measurable and is smaller than the word "incoherent" suggests: the body is rebuilt from the
; database on every request before the map is consulted, and the digest is a pure function of
; that body, so a replica that has never seen a key answers with a full, fresh body carrying the
; same tag. The cost of a replica miss is bandwidth, not a wrong answer.
; --
; WHERE THE SECOND BRIEF IS REFUTED, once, and it is the load-bearing half of the rule it asked
; for. The brief that added the published rule below asked this file to declare that a cached
; figure carrying no timestamp is NOT SERVABLE. The brain rules the opposite for the same class of
; value: brain:knowledge_base:864-866 says the cache is not switched off, that under a silent
; source silence is the worse failure, that what changes is not the number but WHAT IT CALLS
; ITSELF, and that with no date a figure says "time unknown" rather than "probably recent".
; Withholding has one published cause and the brain names it once -- no cache at all. The brief
; collapsed two different states into one: a figure with no DATE (published: show it, and let it
; say the time is unknown) and a figure with no LABEL AT ALL (not published, because that cash
; desk always had somewhere to put the words). This catalog's body is the second state and nothing
; else, so the withhold that follows from it is declared as this file's own derivation, with the
; published rule left saying what it actually says.
; --
; WHAT THIS FILE NEVER DOES. It declares no HTTP status code, no header name and no header
; string. It invents no cache size, no entry ceiling and no eviction rule: the code sets none, the
; brain publishes none either -- searched 2026-09-21 across all nine dumped nodes, one near miss
; named below with the rest -- and the refusal is declared as a named boolean with a note rather
; than filled with a plausible number. It sets no time-to-live on a map entry, because src/api/cache.rs sets none -- the only
; lifetime an entry has is the process. It copies no other surface's age budget: src/main.rs
; sets its own at :819, :834, :1016-1024, :1066 and :1093 for surfaces this contract does not
; own, and turbobaby/upload-media owns the stored-object read header, in
; READ_RESPONSE_IS_BROWSER_CACHEABLE (re-opened and confirmed 2026-09-21). It states no collision
; probability for the digest. Since 2026-09-21 src/api/cache.rs:95-116 does ANNOUNCE the entry
; count each time it crosses a decade, and this file declares that announcement rather than
; reading it as a ceiling: a log line changes nothing about what the map will accept, so the
; refusal above stands unweakened and the growth simply stopped being invisible. It carries no
; price, no rate, no unit count, no family key and no deposit. It carries nothing from D14 -- no
; renter, handle, phone, key code, plate, debt, purchase cost or personal note -- in a constant,
; a fixture or a comment.
; phi^2 + 1/phi^2 = 3 | TRINITY

module turbobaby-http-cache;

pub const KIND : str = "cache-contract";
pub const ID : str = "turbobaby/http-cache";
pub const NAME : str = "TurboBaby catalog cache: derivation, lifetime, and the age of a price";
pub const WORLD : str = "gHashTag/turbobaby-user-bot";
pub const MEASURED_AT : str = "2026-09-21";
pub const IMPLEMENTATION : str = "src/api/cache.rs";
pub const CALL_SITE : str = "src/api/bikes.rs";
pub const DEPLOY_MANIFEST : str = "railway.toml";
pub const DECISION : str = "D11";
pub const DOOR_ISSUE : str = "#25";
; 150 lines and 5 unit tests until 2026-09-21; the ETag repair below added the growth notice,
; the entry count and three tests.
pub const IMPLEMENTATION_LINES : u16 = 314;
pub const IMPLEMENTATION_UNIT_TESTS : u8 = 8;
pub const IMPLEMENTATION_LINES_BEFORE_REPAIR : u16 = 150;
pub const IMPLEMENTATION_UNIT_TESTS_BEFORE_REPAIR : u8 = 5;

; --- Ownership edges -----------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Five owners, five abstract parameters, nothing restated. The first edge is the one a reader is
; most likely to collapse, so it carries named refusals and not only an ID string.
pub const REUSES_CATALOG_API_ID : str = "turbobaby/catalog-api";
pub const THIS_FILE_DECLARES_NO_HTTP_STATUS : bool = true;
pub const THE_CONDITIONAL_OUTCOME_IS_OWNED_THERE : bool = true;
pub const REUSES_PRICING_HONESTY_ID : str = "turbobaby/pricing-honesty";
pub const THE_DOOR_IS_THE_PRICE_AUTHORITY_AND_IT_IS_OWNED_THERE : bool = true;
pub const A_CACHE_MAY_BECOME_THE_PRICE_AUTHORITY : bool = false;
pub const REUSES_AVAILABILITY_ID : str = "turbobaby/availability";
pub const WHAT_CONFIRMS_A_UNIT_COUNT_IS_OWNED_THERE : bool = true;
pub const REUSES_LEGACY_RETIREMENT_ID : str = "turbobaby/legacy-retirement";
pub const THE_RETIREMENT_CENSUS_IS_OWNED_THERE : bool = true;
pub const NEIGHBOUR_RATE_LIMIT_ID : str = "turbobaby/rate-limit";
pub const THE_ONLY_BOUND_ON_KEY_GROWTH_IS_OWNED_THERE : bool = true;
pub const REUSE_EDGE_COUNT : u8 = 5;

; --- The boundary census, re-measured 2026-09-21 ------------------------------------------------
; The header claims three boundaries no other contract occupies. An absence is the one claim a
; reader trusts without re-running it, so the counts it rests on are declared here with the date
; they were taken and the corpus size they were taken over. Two of the three have a near miss,
; and a near miss counted is worth more than an absence asserted.
pub const BOUNDARY_CENSUS_AT : str = "2026-09-21";
pub const CORPUS_FILES_SEARCHED : u8 = 43;
pub const BOUNDARIES_OWNED_HERE : u8 = 3;
pub const CONTRACTS_MENTIONING_AN_ENTITY_TAG : u8 = 2;
pub const CONTRACTS_DECLARING_A_MAX_AGE : u8 = 2;
pub const CONTRACTS_RELATING_AN_AGE_TO_A_PRICE : u8 = 1;
pub const OTHER_MAX_AGE_OWNER_ID : str = "turbobaby/request-identity";
pub const OTHER_ENTITY_TAG_MENTIONER_ID : str = "turbobaby/catalog-api";

; --- What the tag is computed over ---------------------------------------------------------------
; src/api/cache.rs:32-51. SHA-256 over the response body string; the WHOLE thirty-two-byte
; digest is kept and rendered as sixty-four lowercase hex characters by hex::encode. The docstring
; at :7-19 states why a stable hash was chosen over the standard-library hasher. Nothing else is
; hashed: not the cache key, not a request header, not the query. The selection is carried by the
; KEY instead (src/api/bikes.rs:489-498), which is a different mechanism with a different failure.
; WHAT CHANGED, AND WHEN. Until 2026-09-21 this function kept hash[0..8] and rendered sixteen
; characters, so 24 of the 32 bytes were discarded and the tag was 64 bits wide. The measurement
; is preserved below as TAG_KEPT_BYTES_BEFORE_REPAIR rather than overwritten, because the old
; width is what every claim in this file about a stale confirmation used to rest on: at 64 bits a
; birthday collision arrives around 2^32 distinct bodies, which is a number a catalog can reach,
; and the consequence -- a changed body answered as unchanged -- is the one way the re-hash
; guarantee could fail. Nothing was bought by the truncation: the same format! call, 48 more bytes
; per entry. The repair landed with a unit test anchored on FIPS 180-4's own worked examples
; (src/api/cache.rs:208-219) rather than on a second call into sha2, because a test that computes
; its subject the way its subject does would have stayed green through the truncation too.
pub const HASH_ALGORITHM : str = "sha256";
pub const BITS_PER_BYTE : u16 = 8;
pub const HEX_CHARS_PER_BYTE : u16 = 2;
pub const DIGEST_BYTES : u16 = 32;
pub const TAG_KEPT_BYTES : u16 = 32;
pub const TAG_KEPT_BYTES_BEFORE_REPAIR : u16 = 8;
pub const TAG_WIDTH_REPAIRED_AT : str = "2026-09-21";
pub const TAG_HASHES_THE_BODY : bool = true;
pub const TAG_HASHES_THE_KEY : bool = false;
pub const TAG_HASHES_A_HEADER : bool = false;

pub fn tag_bits_kept(kept_bytes: u16) u16 {
  return kept_bytes * BITS_PER_BYTE;
}

pub fn tag_hex_chars(kept_bytes: u16) u16 {
  return kept_bytes * HEX_CHARS_PER_BYTE;
}

pub fn tag_bytes_discarded(digest_bytes: u16, kept_bytes: u16) u16 {
  return digest_bytes - kept_bytes;
}

pub fn tag_depends_only_on_the_body(body_hashed: bool, key_hashed: bool, header_hashed: bool) bool {
  return body_hashed and !key_hashed and !header_hashed;
}

; A consequence of hashing the body alone: two different keys holding byte-identical bodies hold
; the identical tag. That is not the defect it sounds like, because the conditional answer below
; also requires the tag to equal the CURRENT body's hash -- so a tag crossing between keys is
; honoured only when the two bodies really are the same bytes. The guard that keeps distinct
; selections in distinct keys is a unit test at src/api/bikes.rs:2688-2713.
pub fn two_keys_can_share_a_tag(bodies_are_byte_identical: bool) bool {
  return bodies_are_byte_identical;
}

; A collision makes both the stored digest and the fresh digest the same value, so a genuinely
; changed body reads as unchanged. That mechanism is stated here and its rate is still refused.
; What changed on 2026-09-21 is the width it operates at, not the mechanism: the predicate below
; takes the collision as a parameter and is written over no width at all, which is why the repair
; needed no edit to it.
pub const COLLISION_RATE_IS_MEASURED : bool = false;
pub const COLLISION_RATE_NOTE : str = "Nothing in this repository measures, bounds or monitors a collision on this digest, and that was equally true when the digest was truncated to eight bytes -- so the truncation was a hole nobody was watching, rather than a risk somebody had priced. src/api/cache.rs:49-51 now keeps all 32 bytes. A probability written here would be arithmetic about a hash function, not a measurement of this system, so the rate stays declared absent. What IS measured is the width, before and after, and the consequence -- all pinned above and below.";

pub fn changed_body_reads_as_unchanged(body_changed: bool, truncated_digest_collided: bool) bool {
  return body_changed and truncated_digest_collided;
}

; --- What the origin remembers -------------------------------------------------------------------
; src/api/cache.rs:22 -- a map from key to digest behind a read-write lock. No body is stored.
; src/api/cache.rs:118-128 -- the current body is re-hashed on EVERY call, so the stored digest
; never outlives the body it describes. src/api/bikes.rs:478 builds that body from the database
; before :499 consults the map, so a served full body is always freshly built.
pub const ORIGIN_REMEMBERS_A_DIGEST : bool = true;
pub const ORIGIN_REMEMBERS_A_BODY : bool = false;
pub const ORIGIN_REHASHES_EVERY_CALL : bool = true;
pub const BODY_IS_REBUILT_PER_REQUEST : bool = true;

; Local discriminants for what the call site does. They are NOT status codes; catalog_api.t27
; owns those in catalog_api_list_status and this file names no HTTP number.
pub const OUTCOME_NOT_MODIFIED : u8 = 1;
pub const OUTCOME_FULL_BODY : u8 = 2;
pub const OUTCOME_KIND_COUNT : u8 = 2;

; src/api/bikes.rs:499-510. Three conditions, all required: the key is present on THIS process's
; map, its stored digest equals the fresh digest of the current body, and the client's supplied
; tag equals that same digest. An absent key makes the first condition false, which is the
; replica case below.
pub fn may_answer_not_modified(key_present_here: bool, stored_digest_matches: bool, client_tag_matches: bool) bool {
  return key_present_here and stored_digest_matches and client_tag_matches;
}

pub fn outcome_kind(key_present_here: bool, stored_digest_matches: bool, client_tag_matches: bool) u8 {
  if (may_answer_not_modified(key_present_here, stored_digest_matches, client_tag_matches)) {
    return OUTCOME_NOT_MODIFIED;
  }
  return OUTCOME_FULL_BODY;
}

; The origin can answer "unchanged" about a body that changed in exactly one way: a collision.
; Absent re-hashing it could also do so through a missing invalidator, which is why the
; invalidation parameter stays in the signature rather than being dropped as dead today.
pub fn origin_can_confirm_a_stale_body(rehashes_every_call: bool, key_has_a_live_invalidator: bool, truncated_digest_collided: bool) bool {
  if (truncated_digest_collided) {
    return true;
  }
  if (rehashes_every_call) {
    return false;
  }
  return !key_has_a_live_invalidator;
}

; --- Invalidation --------------------------------------------------------------------------------
; Four functions, five key names, and no overlap with the keys a live writer creates.
pub const INVALIDATOR_FUNCTIONS : u8 = 4;
pub const INVALIDATOR_KEY_NAMES : u8 = 5;
pub const INVALIDATORS_ANNOTATED_DEAD : u8 = 3;
pub const INVALIDATOR_CALL_SITES : u8 = 1;
pub const INVALIDATORS_NAMING_A_LIVE_KEY : u8 = 0;
pub const LIVE_KEY_BASES : [2]str = ["bikes", "bikes_available"];
pub const LIVE_KEY_BASE_COUNT : u8 = 2;
pub const INVALIDATION_NOTE : str = "The four invalidators are at src/api/cache.rs:160-164, :168-170, :174-176 and :180-182; the last three carry a dead-code annotation. The single call site is src/api/admin.rs:686 and it clears keys for a route src/ui/api/client.rs:119-121 records as retired by migration 083; legacy_retirement.t27 owns that census. The live writer at src/api/bikes.rs:489-499 creates keys under the two bases above. Coverage of a live key is therefore zero, and harmless, because the origin re-hashes.";

pub fn a_write_can_clear_this_key(key_is_named_by_an_invalidator: bool, a_call_site_exists: bool) bool {
  return key_is_named_by_an_invalidator and a_call_site_exists;
}

; An entry dies two ways and no others. There is no expiry timer on a map entry anywhere in
; src/api/cache.rs, so an entry that is neither removed nor lost to a restart lives forever. The
; growth notice added on 2026-09-21 does not change this: it counts, it does not expire.
pub const ENTRY_HAS_A_TIMER : bool = false;
pub const ENTRY_LIFETIME : str = "the process";

pub fn entry_is_gone(process_restarted: bool, key_removed: bool) bool {
  return process_restarted or key_removed;
}

; --- Size: still unbounded, no longer unseen -------------------------------------------------------
; src/api/cache.rs holds a plain map: no capacity, no eviction. The key space is shaped by the
; caller, not by the fleet: src/api/bikes.rs:381-395 folds the class and both displacement bounds
; into the key, and the parser at :309-337 rejects only a non-integer, a negative value and an
; inverted band. Every remaining non-negative integer is a distinct key. The class term is the one
; closed dimension -- src/api/bikes.rs:944 declares two values and the parser at :300 admits no
; third -- and two values do not bound a product with two open ones.
; WHAT CHANGED ON 2026-09-21, AND WHAT DID NOT. Until then the map had no accounting either: the
; entry count could not be read from anywhere, so growth was not merely unbounded but invisible,
; and the first evidence of it would have been the process's memory. src/api/cache.rs:67-69 now
; reports the count and :104-114 writes it to the log the first time it reaches 10, 100, 1000 and
; so on. A decade is a reporting cadence and NOT a threshold -- nothing refuses, nothing is
; evicted, no behaviour differs on either side of it -- which is exactly why it could be chosen
; here without an owner: it states a fact rather than setting a policy. A capacity could not have
; been chosen the same way. None is published anywhere in this repository, the nearest bound is
; turbobaby/rate-limit's, which bounds attempts and not distinct keys, and a number invented here
; would have been read as the owner's ceiling by everyone who came after. So the two booleans
; below still read false, and the third one is what is new.
pub const ENTRY_LIMIT_IS_SET : bool = false;
pub const EVICTION_RULE_EXISTS : bool = false;
pub const GROWTH_IS_ANNOUNCED : bool = true;
pub const GROWTH_NOTICE_CADENCE : str = "each decade: 10, 100, 1000, and so on";
pub const GROWTH_NOTICE_FIRST_AT : u16 = 10;
pub const GROWTH_NOTICE_IS_A_CEILING : bool = false;
pub const ENTRY_COUNT_IS_READABLE : bool = true;
pub const NOT_A_COUNT : i32 = -1;
pub const MAX_ENTRIES : i32 = NOT_A_COUNT;
pub const CLASS_VALUES_ACCEPTED : u8 = 2;
pub const CLASS_VALUE_SET_IS_CLOSED : bool = true;
pub const CC_BOUND_LOW_ACCEPTED : i32 = 0;
pub const CC_BOUND_IS_CAPPED : bool = false;
pub const SIZE_REFUSAL_NOTE : str = "No entry ceiling exists to pin. MAX_ENTRIES carries the NOT_A_COUNT sentinel rather than a plausible figure, because a figure here would be an invention and because the honest statement is stronger: the number of distinct keys is a function of caller-supplied integers, and the only bound on how many a caller may ask for lives in rate_limit.t27 rather than here. Since 2026-09-21 the count is at least VISIBLE -- src/api/cache.rs:67-69 reports it and :104-114 logs it at each decade -- and the sentinel is unchanged by that, because a measurement is not a maximum.";

pub fn map_entries_are_bounded(entry_limit_is_set: bool, eviction_rule_exists: bool) bool {
  return entry_limit_is_set or eviction_rule_exists;
}

; Bounded and observable are different questions, and answering the second one yes must never be
; allowed to read as yes to the first. Two functions, never one.
pub fn growth_is_observable(entry_limit_is_set: bool, eviction_rule_exists: bool, growth_is_announced: bool) bool {
  return entry_limit_is_set or eviction_rule_exists or growth_is_announced;
}

pub fn key_space_is_bounded_by_the_fleet(cc_bound_is_capped: bool, class_value_set_is_closed: bool) bool {
  return cc_bound_is_capped and class_value_set_is_closed;
}

; --- How long a copy may be held -----------------------------------------------------------------
; src/api/bikes.rs:516-519. The directive is public with an age budget of 60 seconds and carries
; no revalidation demand. Within that budget a client is entitled to answer from its own copy and
; never reach the origin, so the conditional machinery above is not consulted at all.
pub const CATALOG_MAX_AGE_SECONDS : u32 = 60;
pub const RESPONSE_IS_PUBLICLY_CACHEABLE : bool = true;
pub const MUST_REVALIDATE_IS_SET : bool = false;
pub const NO_CACHE_IS_SET : bool = false;
pub const THIS_FILE_COPIES_ANOTHER_SURFACES_BUDGET : bool = false;

pub fn client_may_reuse_without_asking(age_seconds: u32, max_age_seconds: u32, must_revalidate: bool) bool {
  if (must_revalidate) {
    return false;
  }
  return age_seconds < max_age_seconds;
}

pub fn conditional_request_is_reached(age_seconds: u32, max_age_seconds: u32, must_revalidate: bool) bool {
  return !client_may_reuse_without_asking(age_seconds, max_age_seconds, must_revalidate);
}

pub fn last_reusable_age_seconds(max_age_seconds: u32) u32 {
  return max_age_seconds - 1;
}

; A copy already in a client's hands is recalled by exactly one thing: a rule that forces the
; client to ask. Removing a key from the origin map is not such a rule, and believing it is one
; is the single most expensive misreading available here.
pub const REMOVING_A_KEY_RECALLS_A_HELD_COPY : bool = false;

pub fn held_copy_is_recallable(must_revalidate_was_sent: bool) bool {
  return must_revalidate_was_sent;
}

; The age of what a reader is looking at is additive: how old the origin's own answer was when it
; was baked into the body, plus how long the copy has been held.
pub fn served_copy_age_seconds(origin_answer_age_seconds: u32, held_seconds: u32) u32 {
  return origin_answer_age_seconds + held_seconds;
}

pub fn worst_case_served_age_seconds(origin_answer_age_seconds: u32, max_age_seconds: u32) u32 {
  return served_copy_age_seconds(origin_answer_age_seconds, last_reusable_age_seconds(max_age_seconds));
}

; The first term of that sum is not measured. src/api/bikes.rs:250-253 answers every key with
; silence, so there is no door answer to have an age yet; DECISIONS.md:168 records one door call
; at 21.4 seconds, which is a latency and not the age of an answer once it is held.
pub const ORIGIN_ANSWER_AGE_IS_MEASURED : bool = false;
pub const ORIGIN_ANSWER_AGE_NOTE : str = "How stale the door's own answer is by the time it is serialized has no measurement in this repository. The one door number that exists, data/fleet_seed.json:95 via src/api/bikes.rs:236-237, is a round-trip latency of 21.4 seconds, which bounds how long a request waits and says nothing about how old the value returned already was. The term is therefore an abstract parameter here and not a constant.";

; --- The age of a price (D11) ---------------------------------------------------------------------
; pricing_honesty.t27 owns the rule that the door is the authority. The only thing this file adds
; is arithmetic the door rule cannot see: authority is a property of an answer NOW, and a copy
; with a positive age is not that.
pub const DOOR_IS_WIRED : bool = false;
pub const DOOR_STUB_NOTE : str = "src/api/bikes.rs:250-253 discards its argument and answers with an empty map, which the docstring at :229-249 states is the same shape a wired door must produce when the owner does not answer. So no door number is in any cached body today, and the price-age window described here is armed and empty rather than live.";

pub fn body_can_carry_a_door_price(door_is_wired: bool) bool {
  return door_is_wired;
}

pub fn price_in_a_served_body_is_authoritative(door_answered_at_origin: bool, age_seconds: u32) bool {
  if (!door_answered_at_origin) {
    return false;
  }
  return age_seconds == 0;
}

pub fn stale_door_price_is_reachable(door_is_wired: bool, max_age_seconds: u32) bool {
  return door_is_wired and max_age_seconds > 0;
}

; The honest refusal, made executable. Nobody has decided what price age is acceptable, so the
; verdict has three outcomes and the undecided one is not a pass. A zero-second copy under an
; undecided policy still returns undecided: the age is not the question until the policy exists.
; READ THIS REFUSAL AT ITS OWN SIZE. It is about a CEILING IN SECONDS and about nothing else.
; What a figure of any age must call itself HAS been ruled on, and that rule is published in the
; brain and declared in the section after this one; it changes no constant here, because a label
; is not a ceiling and neither one answers the other's question.
pub const AGE_UNDECIDED : u8 = 0;
pub const AGE_ACCEPTABLE : u8 = 1;
pub const AGE_TOO_OLD : u8 = 2;
pub const AGE_VERDICT_COUNT : u8 = 3;
pub const ACCEPTABLE_PRICE_AGE_IS_DECIDED : bool = false;
pub const NOT_A_DURATION_SECONDS : i32 = -1;
pub const ACCEPTABLE_PRICE_AGE_SECONDS : i32 = NOT_A_DURATION_SECONDS;
pub const ACCEPTABLE_PRICE_AGE_NOTE : str = "D11 at DECISIONS.md:156-170 rules that the door is the authority, that silence must not be filled, and that divergence is logged; it sets no maximum age for a number already published. The 60 at src/api/bikes.rs:518 is a transport budget chosen for a body that contained no price, not a freshness decision about one. src/api/bikes.rs:483-488 records the choice as explicitly deferred to issue #25. The sentinel above says so rather than a number saying otherwise.";

pub fn acceptable_price_age_seconds() i32 {
  return ACCEPTABLE_PRICE_AGE_SECONDS;
}

pub fn age_verdict(policy_is_decided: bool, age_seconds: u32, acceptable_age_seconds: u32) u8 {
  if (!policy_is_decided) {
    return AGE_UNDECIDED;
  }
  if (age_seconds <= acceptable_age_seconds) {
    return AGE_ACCEPTABLE;
  }
  return AGE_TOO_OLD;
}

; --- What a number from a cache must call itself (decided 2026-08-13, read 2026-09-21) -----------
; The question the section above leaves open -- what makes a number with an age fit to show -- has
; an answer, and the answer is not in this repository. The owner's knowledge base publishes it and
; this file cites that base the way it cites a source file, by node and line.
;
; THE INCIDENT IT CAME FROM (brain:knowledge_base:857-859). On 13 August 2026 the bridge behind a
; cash desk in the owner's other system answered with nothing after three attempts, and what the
; group then read carried TWO claims about the world -- "I wrote it" and "the balance is this" --
; both made without grounds: a figure of 1529 was shown twice while the sheet held 1029. Neither
; number is a price, a rate or a deposit; they are a till balance in another system, carried here
; as the evidence a rule stands on and not as anything this catalog serves. The gap is 500. Which
; of the two expenses named beside it, 120 and 500, the stale copy predated is NOT published, so
; this file does not say either.
;
; THE RULE (brain:knowledge_base:860-866), translated and not summarised. A number has THREE
; outcomes and not two. Recomputed from the source -> the ordinary answer. The source is silent
; and a cache exists -> the number IS shown, marked NOT RECONCILED, together with the date on
; which it was true. No cache -> no number at all, because a zero in place of a balance lies
; louder than silence. And the sentence that generalises it, which is the reason the rule reaches
; a catalog price at all: what changes is not the number but WHAT IT CALLS ITSELF, and with no
; date it is "time unknown", never "probably recent".
;
; WHICH OUTCOME IS CHOSEN BY WHAT. By whether the source ANSWERED, never by how the number looks
; (brain:knowledge_base:867-869 records the earlier version of this rule judging the truthfulness
; of the value instead, and confusing an empty till with a sick bridge in both directions). A zero
; a live source answered with is an honest zero and takes the ordinary branch.
;
; WHAT KIND OF FACT THIS IS, kept separate on purpose. The rule is a RULING IN FORCE, dated
; 13 August 2026, and it is not a measurement. CORRECTED 2026-09-21 BY REVIEW: this sentence
; read "the rule is the owner's decision", and the node does not say that. Node knowledge_base
; uses no on-the-owner's-word marker at all -- zero lines of its 1114 carry one -- while that
; marker is node business_rules's, which opens by saying the owner or the managers change it
; (brain:business_rules:1-3) and marks each such rule where it stands (brain:business_rules:798
; and :1364 are two of its 28 marked lines). brain:knowledge_base:856-877 has instead the shape
; of an engineering decision record: an incident, "what applies now", a census, and a module
; with its guard, its tests and its rollback switch (brain:knowledge_base:876). So the author
; is UNNAMED at the source and is left unnamed here. Nothing the withdrawn claim carried is
; lost: the rule is still not a measurement, and the measurement lying beside it in the same node
; (brain:knowledge_base:875) is a different fact in different constants: over 73 days that cash
; desk answered with a figure 320 times, 319 of them recomputed live and once from the cache. That
; census is about that system. It is not about this one, the frequency here is not measured at
; all, and what IS measurable here is that the branch cannot fire today at all, because the door
; is a stub.
;
; WHAT THE RULE DOES NOT DECIDE. It sets no ceiling in seconds, so ACCEPTABLE_PRICE_AGE_SECONDS
; keeps its sentinel and ACCEPTABLE_PRICE_AGE_IS_DECIDED stays false: what was ruled is that
; the ceiling is not what governs, the label is. Nor does it turn a published figure into a client
; quote. D11 is untouched, the door is still the price authority, turbobaby/pricing-honesty still
; owns that rule, and what is declared here is what the shop PUBLISHES about how a number must
; present itself -- not what anybody may charge for a bike.
;
; THE NEIGHBOUR, by ID and declaration name. turbobaby/pricing-honesty owns the spatial half:
; render() maps an absent rate to SHAPE_DASH and never to a zero, and rate_zero_filled_record() is
; the D9 case that rule exists to refuse. This section is its TEMPORAL TWIN -- an absent time is
; not a recent time, exactly as an absent rate is not a zero -- and it adds no second renderer.
; The wording, and the languages it has to reach a reader in, belong to the surface;
; turbobaby/locale-policy holds copy and is named here as a bystander and not as an ownership
; edge, because this file takes no declaration from it and the census of five above is unchanged.
;
; A SECOND NODE SAYS IT FROM TWO OTHER DIRECTIONS, and one of the two is a near miss for a refusal
; this file keeps. brain:business_rules is the owner's rule book, moved into the brain on
; 01.08.2026; its cash-desk section is four lines -- a heading and two rules -- and both rules
; land here.
; brain:business_rules:71 rules that the balance is NOT STORED and is recomputed from the sheet's
; rows -- which is, for another system and by another route, exactly what
; ORIGIN_REMEMBERS_A_BODY == false and BODY_IS_REBUILT_PER_REQUEST == true already say about this
; one: correctness comes from recomputing, and a cache is only ever the answer of last resort.
; brain:business_rules:72-73 rules that a balance must be recorded with EVERY currency in one
; message, because naming a subset drops a currency from the cache. That is the nearest thing to
; an eviction rule in the whole brain and it is not one: it says what a partial write does to one
; record, not when a record dies or how many may live. The refusal stands and the near miss is
; counted, which this file already holds to be worth more than an absence asserted.
;
; AND THE GENERAL RULE THE OWNER WROTE FIRST, about a service cell and not about money at all:
; brain:business_rules:32-34 rules that an empty cell means NOT MEASURED -- neither "overdue" nor
; "zero" -- and that the three outcomes are named separately. So this rule has four statements:
; two the owner's, a service cell and a cash balance, and two this repository's, D9 at
; DECISIONS.md:116-129 and pricing_honesty.t27's dash. The section here is the time dimension of
; the same thing, and it was the last of the four to be written down.

; The three outcomes, as published. They belong to a NUMBER and not to a response: the two
; outcomes this file already owns are OUTCOME_NOT_MODIFIED and OUTCOME_FULL_BODY, a different
; subject entirely. They are numbered from eleven so that no value of one census can be read as a
; value of the other, and the tests below assert the disjointness rather than trusting the gap.
; The gap means nothing else; eleven is not a quantity of anything.
pub const FIGURE_RECOMPUTED : u8 = 11;
pub const FIGURE_UNRECONCILED : u8 = 12;
pub const FIGURE_WITHHELD : u8 = 13;
pub const FIGURE_OUTCOME_COUNT : u8 = 3;

; What the number calls itself: two obligations, not one -- WHAT it is, and WHEN it was true.
; TIME_LABEL_NONE is no label at all, which is a different state from a label that says the time
; is unknown, and the difference is the whole of the second brief's error. TIME_LABEL_PROBABLY_-
; RECENT exists so the forbidden one has a name a test can refuse by; nothing here ever returns it.
pub const TIME_LABEL_NONE : u8 = 0;
pub const TIME_LABEL_DATED : u8 = 1;
pub const TIME_LABEL_UNKNOWN : u8 = 2;
pub const TIME_LABEL_PROBABLY_RECENT : u8 = 3;
; The obligation in English, not the wording: the published strings are Russian and Thai, this
; corpus is ASCII, and the surface says it in the reader's language. Same division of labour as
; pricing_honesty.t27's MUST_SAY.
pub const SAY_NOT_RECONCILED : str = "not reconciled";
pub const SAY_TIME_UNKNOWN : str = "time unknown";
pub const MUST_NOT_SAY : str = "probably recent";
pub const LABEL_WORDING_IS_OWNED_HERE : bool = false;

; Provenance of the rule, held apart from provenance of the measurement beside it, because a rule
; is a different kind of fact from a number read off data. WHOSE rule it is is a third question
; and the node does not answer it: see the correction above. The withdrawn claim is kept as a
; constant so a reader can tell a retracted attribution from one that was never made.
pub const RULE_SOURCE : str = "brain:knowledge_base:856-866";
pub const RULE_CITATION_FORM : str = "brain:<node>:<line>";
pub const RULE_SOURCE_IS_IN_THIS_REPOSITORY : bool = false;
pub const RULE_DECIDED_AT : str = "2026-08-13";
pub const RULE_READ_AT : str = "2026-09-21";
pub const RULE_IS_A_RULING_IN_FORCE : bool = true;
pub const RULE_AUTHOR_IS_NAMED_IN_THE_NODE : bool = false;
pub const RULE_NODE_OWNER_WORD_MARKERS : u8 = 0;
pub const RULE_WAS_DECLARED_THE_OWNERS_DECISION_UNTIL : str = "2026-09-21";
pub const RULE_IS_A_MEASUREMENT : bool = false;
pub const BRAIN_CITERS_IN_THE_CORPUS_ARE_COUNTED : bool = false;
pub const BRAIN_CITERS_NOTE : str = "How many other contracts cite this knowledge base is not declared here. Sibling files were under edit in the same hour as this one, so a corpus count taken now would be a reading of a moving corpus rather than a fact, and every other census in this file is re-runnable in a way that one would not be.";

pub const INCIDENT_SOURCE : str = "brain:knowledge_base:857-859";
pub const INCIDENT_AT : str = "2026-08-13";
pub const INCIDENT_ATTEMPTS_BEFORE_SILENCE : u8 = 3;
pub const INCIDENT_UNGROUNDED_CLAIMS : u8 = 2;
pub const INCIDENT_TIMES_THE_FIGURE_WAS_SHOWN : u8 = 2;
pub const INCIDENT_FIGURE_SHOWN : u16 = 1529;
pub const INCIDENT_FIGURE_RECONCILED : u16 = 1029;
pub const INCIDENT_FIGURES_ARE_A_TILL_BALANCE : bool = true;
pub const INCIDENT_FIGURES_ARE_A_PRICE_OR_A_RATE : bool = false;
pub const WHICH_EXPENSE_THE_COPY_PREDATED_IS_PUBLISHED : bool = false;

pub const CENSUS_SOURCE : str = "brain:knowledge_base:875";
pub const CENSUS_DAYS : u16 = 73;
pub const CENSUS_ANSWERS_WITH_A_FIGURE : u16 = 320;
pub const CENSUS_RECOMPUTED : u16 = 319;
pub const CENSUS_FROM_CACHE : u16 = 1;
pub const CENSUS_IS_ABOUT_THIS_REPOSITORY : bool = false;

pub fn figure_gap(shown: u16, reconciled: u16) u16 {
  return shown - reconciled;
}

pub fn census_adds_up(answers: u16, recomputed: u16, from_cache: u16) bool {
  return answers == recomputed + from_cache;
}

; The published selector. It reads the ANSWER and never the value.
pub fn figure_outcome(source_answered: bool, cache_exists: bool) u8 {
  if (source_answered) {
    return FIGURE_RECOMPUTED;
  }
  if (cache_exists) {
    return FIGURE_UNRECONCILED;
  }
  return FIGURE_WITHHELD;
}

pub fn zero_from_a_live_source_is_a_fact(source_answered: bool, figure_is_zero: bool) bool {
  return source_answered and figure_is_zero;
}

; The time half of the label. A figure that carries no date is still served -- as "time unknown".
pub fn time_label(figure_carries_a_date: bool) u8 {
  if (figure_carries_a_date) {
    return TIME_LABEL_DATED;
  }
  return TIME_LABEL_UNKNOWN;
}

pub fn time_label_is_permitted(label: u8) bool {
  return label == TIME_LABEL_DATED or label == TIME_LABEL_UNKNOWN;
}

; What may reach a reader. A recomputed figure goes bare; an unreconciled one only while it says
; both what it is and when it was true; a withheld one never, and nothing stands in for it.
pub fn figure_may_be_shown(outcome: u8, says_not_reconciled: bool, label: u8) bool {
  if (outcome == FIGURE_WITHHELD) {
    return false;
  }
  if (outcome == FIGURE_RECOMPUTED) {
    return true;
  }
  return says_not_reconciled and time_label_is_permitted(label);
}

; D9 arriving from the other direction. pricing_honesty.t27 refuses a zero standing in for a rate
; nobody published; this refuses one standing in for a figure nobody could reconcile.
pub const ZERO_STANDS_IN_FOR_A_WITHHELD_FIGURE : bool = false;

pub fn zero_may_stand_in_for_a_withheld_figure() bool {
  return ZERO_STANDS_IN_FOR_A_WITHHELD_FIGURE;
}

; A label does not promote a number. An unreconciled figure is honest and is still not the door's
; answer NOW -- which is the rule pricing_honesty.t27 owns and this file only composes with.
pub fn served_figure_is_authoritative(outcome: u8, door_answered_at_origin: bool, age_seconds: u32) bool {
  if (outcome != FIGURE_RECOMPUTED) {
    return false;
  }
  return price_in_a_served_body_is_authoritative(door_answered_at_origin, age_seconds);
}

; Can this transport obey the rule today? Measured 2026-09-21. src/api/bikes.rs:478 builds the body
; as one object of families; a family says WHERE its number came from -- src/api/bikes.rs:641
; inserts client_rate_source, whose two values are declared at :178 and :184 -- and says nowhere
; WHEN it was true: src/db/bikes.rs:107 and the struct it flattens carry no time field, and the
; body carries no envelope. So the body can state one half of the label and not the other, and the
; published rule needs a third source value ("not reconciled") that the two above do not include.
pub const BODY_CARRIES_A_SOURCE_FIELD : bool = true;
pub const BODY_CARRIES_A_TIME_FIELD : bool = false;
pub const BODY_SOURCE_FIELD : str = "client_rate_source";
pub const BODY_SOURCE_FIELD_VALUES : [2]str = ["door", "unavailable"];
pub const BODY_SOURCE_FIELD_VALUE_COUNT : u8 = 2;
pub const RULE_NEEDS_A_SOURCE_VALUE_THAT_DOES_NOT_EXIST_YET : bool = true;

pub fn body_can_carry_the_published_label(source_field_exists: bool, time_field_exists: bool) bool {
  return source_field_exists and time_field_exists;
}

; DERIVED HERE, AND SAID SO. The brain publishes one withhold and names its cause once: no cache.
; It publishes no withhold for a missing DATE -- a dateless figure is shown, saying the time is
; unknown. This file adds a second cause for a case that cash desk does not have: a transport with
; no field to carry any label at all. A bare number in a body claims to be current, the rule
; forbids the claim, and the only honest move left is to omit the number -- which is where D9 and
; pricing_honesty.t27's dash arrive from the other direction.
pub const THE_OWNER_PUBLISHED_A_WITHHOLD_FOR_NO_CACHE : bool = true;
pub const THE_OWNER_PUBLISHED_A_WITHHOLD_FOR_A_MISSING_DATE : bool = false;
pub const WITHHOLD_WITHOUT_A_LABEL_FIELD_IS_DERIVED_HERE : bool = true;

pub fn outcome_this_transport_can_honestly_produce(source_answered: bool, cache_exists: bool, label_field_exists: bool) u8 {
  if (source_answered) {
    return FIGURE_RECOMPUTED;
  }
  if (!cache_exists) {
    return FIGURE_WITHHELD;
  }
  if (!label_field_exists) {
    return FIGURE_WITHHELD;
  }
  return FIGURE_UNRECONCILED;
}

; The one local frequency there is. While the door is a stub no figure enters a body, so the
; second outcome is unreachable here today -- armed and empty, exactly like the price-age window
; above, and it arms itself on the same day for the same reason.
pub fn unreconciled_branch_is_reachable(door_is_wired: bool) bool {
  return door_is_wired;
}

; WHAT THIS FILE DECLARED BEFORE 2026-09-21, kept rather than overwritten. The sentence was
; "Nobody has decided what price age is acceptable", and it was true of THIS REPOSITORY when it
; was written: the grep recorded in the WHY block returned 0 lines from DECISIONS.md and the call
; site had deferred the choice to issue #25 in a comment nothing checks. What was wrong with it
; was its reach and not its subject -- it read as though nobody anywhere had decided, while the
; owner had decided in August, in a system this repository does not read. A ceiling in seconds is
; still refused; a label rule is published; they are different questions and stay different
; constants.
pub const AGE_RULE_REFUSAL_REPLACED_AT : str = "2026-09-21";
pub const AGE_RULE_REFUSAL_AS_WRITTEN : str = "Nobody has decided what price age is acceptable, so the verdict has three outcomes and the undecided one is not a pass.";
pub const AGE_RULE_REFUSAL_WAS_TRUE_OF_THIS_REPOSITORY : bool = true;
pub const AGE_RULE_REFUSAL_SEARCHED : str = "DECISIONS.md";
pub const AGE_RULE_REFUSAL_SEARCH_HITS : u8 = 0;
pub const A_SECONDS_CEILING_IS_PUBLISHED : bool = false;
pub const A_LABEL_RULE_IS_PUBLISHED : bool = true;

; The two refusals the brain does not lift either. Searched 2026-09-21 across all nine dumped
; nodes for a capacity, an entry ceiling and an eviction rule: none is published. The one near
; miss is named rather than left for a reader to trip over -- brain:knowledge_base:1007 and :1025
; set a thirty-minute TTL on a confirmation request, which bounds how long a question waits for an
; answer and is not the lifetime of a cached entry, exactly as turbobaby/request-identity's
; AUTH_DATE_MAX_AGE_SECONDS is not the lifetime of a served copy.
pub const BRAIN_NODES_SEARCHED : u8 = 9;
pub const BRAIN_SEARCHED_AT : str = "2026-09-21";
pub const BRAIN_PUBLISHES_AN_ENTRY_CEILING : bool = false;
pub const BRAIN_PUBLISHES_AN_EVICTION_RULE : bool = false;
pub const BRAIN_NEAR_MISS_TTL_MINUTES : u16 = 30;
pub const BRAIN_NEAR_MISS_IS_A_CACHE_TTL : bool = false;
pub const BRAIN_NEAR_MISS_PARTIAL_WRITE : str = "brain:business_rules:72-73";
pub const BRAIN_NEAR_MISS_PARTIAL_WRITE_IS_AN_EVICTION_RULE : bool = false;

; The second node, and what it agrees with rather than adds.
pub const SECOND_RULE_SOURCE : str = "brain:business_rules:71";
pub const SECOND_RULE_NODE_MOVED_AT : str = "2026-08-01";
pub const THE_OWNER_ALSO_RECOMPUTES_RATHER_THAN_STORES : bool = true;
pub const ABSENCE_RULE_SOURCE : str = "brain:business_rules:32-34";
pub const ABSENCE_RULE_OUTCOMES : u8 = 3;
pub const ABSENCE_RULE_STATEMENTS : u8 = 4;
pub const ABSENCE_RULE_STATEMENTS_THE_OWNERS : u8 = 2;
pub const ABSENCE_RULE_STATEMENTS_THIS_REPOSITORYS : u8 = 2;

pub fn statement_census_adds_up(total: u8, owners: u8, ours: u8) bool {
  return total == owners + ours;
}

; --- The age of an availability claim --------------------------------------------------------------
; The same body carries the unit rollup, so the same seconds apply to it. availability.t27 owns
; what may confirm a count; this file only observes that a held copy is never such a check.
pub const A_HELD_COPY_IS_A_LIVE_CHECK : bool = false;

pub fn served_copy_is_a_confirmation(live_check_performed: bool, age_seconds: u32) bool {
  if (age_seconds > 0) {
    return false;
  }
  return live_check_performed;
}

; --- Where the map lives ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
; src/main.rs:715 constructs one map and hands it to the shared state, and it is the only such
; construction in the binary. A grep that counts four has counted the test module: the other three
; ETagCache::new() calls are src/api/cache.rs:252, :289 and :298, all inside that file's cfg(test)
; block at :190. They were two at :125 and :134 before 2026-09-21; the count a grep returns is a
; fact about the test module and moves whenever a test is added. There is no external cache
; dependency in the binary or its manifest --
; grep -rni "redis\|memcached\|moka\|dashmap" Cargo.toml src/ returned 0 on 2026-09-21.
; railway.toml:14-18 declares one region at one replica, so today's coherence is a fact about a
; deployment file and not about this code.
pub const BACKING_STORE : str = "process memory";
pub const MAP_CONSTRUCTIONS_IN_THE_BINARY : u8 = 1;
pub const SHARED_STORE_DEPENDENCIES : u8 = 0;
pub const STORE_IS_SHARED_ACROSS_PROCESSES : bool = false;
pub const DECLARED_REGIONS : u8 = 1;
pub const DECLARED_REPLICAS : u16 = 1;
pub const COHERENCE_IS_A_DEPLOYMENT_FACT : bool = true;
pub const REPLICA_NOTE : str = "The count above is read from railway.toml:18 and is a deployment declaration, not a property of src/. It can change in one edit to a file no test in this repository reads. The contract therefore takes the replica count as a parameter everywhere below and asserts the coherence rule, never the count alone.";
; RE-MEASURED 2026-09-22: "a file no test in this repository reads" is the 2026-09-21 reading.
; scripts/verify_t27_against_source.py now binds DECLARED_REPLICAS to the one numReplicas entry in
; railway.toml, and tests/t27_gates_run.rs runs that gate under cargo test, so an edit to the
; replica count or a second region now fails a test. The contract's stance is unchanged: it still
; takes the replica count as a parameter and asserts the coherence rule, never the count alone.

pub fn map_is_coherent_across_the_fleet(replicas: u16, store_is_shared: bool) bool {
  if (store_is_shared) {
    return true;
  }
  return replicas <= 1;
}

; What a second replica actually costs. The body is rebuilt per request and the digest is a pure
; function of it, so a replica that has never seen a key still answers with a correct body under
; the correct tag; it simply cannot take the short path.
pub fn replica_miss_can_serve_a_wrong_body(body_is_rebuilt_per_request: bool) bool {
  return !body_is_rebuilt_per_request;
}

pub fn outcome_on_a_cold_replica(stored_digest_matches: bool, client_tag_matches: bool) u8 {
  return outcome_kind(false, stored_digest_matches, client_tag_matches);
}

; --- Which surfaces opt out ------------------------------------------------------------------------
; src/api/bikes.rs:1072-1074 gives the owner's edit screen no tag and no key on purpose, with the
; reason stated: an unchanged answer served off a body that predates the last save is how an edit
; appears to have been lost. That is the general rule, not a local quirk.
pub const ADMIN_EDIT_SURFACE_IS_CACHED : bool = false;

pub fn surface_may_be_cached(is_an_edit_surface: bool) bool {
  return !is_an_edit_surface;
}

; --- Tests -------------------------------------------------------------------------------------

; The census behind the header's absence. Each count is over the whole corpus and not over the
; neighbours this file happened to think of; the near misses are asserted as PRESENT, because an
; absence that has never met a near miss has not been measured.
test the_three_boundaries_are_measured_and_the_near_misses_are_named {
  assert BOUNDARIES_OWNED_HERE == 3;
  assert CORPUS_FILES_SEARCHED == 43;
  assert BOUNDARY_CENSUS_AT == "2026-09-21";
